# ==== T-FMT-B — the `"""` COLUMN-TRACKING cell.
# PIN, with its RED-verification honestly reported (Core #12's escape hatch).
#
# SCOPE CLAIM, narrowed to what the file actually contains: ONE cell — a
# multi-line `"""` literal whose closing line is followed by something else, so
# the emitter's column after the literal is observable at all. A verbatim
# multi-line emission goes through the emitter's `write_preformatted` — the
# writer whose contract is "text that may contain newlines", and which resets
# the column from the LAST newline — rather than `write`, which advances the
# column by the whole slice length and so leaves a phantom column of the
# literal's full byte count.
#
# The trailing comment is the SHAPE, not the mechanism. It cannot itself
# desync: trailing comments are injected into the finished buffer and aligned by
# `plan_trailing_aligns`, a pure post-pass that never reads `emitter.col`. The
# column's only consumer is `write_doc`'s fit decision. This fixture does NOT
# sample a multi-line literal nested inside a Doc-laid-out construct — an
# earlier version of this header claimed it did, and no such construct is here.
#
# MEASURED (2026-08-11): swapping the verbatim path to `write` and re-running
# the whole corpus produced BYTE-IDENTICAL output. The emitter's column feeds
# exactly one consumer, `write_doc`'s fit decision, and every Doc-bearing
# construct builds its document from sub-formatter renderings BEFORE the outer
# column is consulted — so the desync has no live observer today. The correct
# writer is used anyway, because "no current observer" is not "correct", and
# this cell stands as the guard for the day one appears. It is a PIN: it cannot
# be made to fail by breaking the mechanism it names.
void main():
    String s = """
alpha
beta
"""    # the closing line carries a trailing comment
    print(s.len())
